The base is zune-jpeg 0.5.15 as crates.io publishes it: the crate's `.cargo_vcs_info.json` names
that commit, its `src/` is byte for byte the published one, and upstream tags no 0.5.15. The paths
below are the crate's own, so the patch applies in the repository with
`git apply --directory=crates/zune-jpeg`. Carried by the owner's fork `close2/zune-image`, branch
`pdf-viewer/0.5.15-with-fixes`, at `1c7d01b8`, which the root Cargo.toml pins: doc/questions/Q227,
answered (ADRs 1589, 1730). The upstream report is the `.md` beside this file.
Found by the `jpeg_bands` fuzz target (ADR 1495); the analysis is ADR 1520. A baseline scan whose
entropy-coded data holds every MCU and ends with no EOI after it (ITU-T T.81 section E.2.3 ends a
scan on its MCU count) is decoded whole except its last MCU row, which comes back 128. The bit
reader's lookahead reaches the end of the data while that row's bits are still in the buffer;
`overread_by` turns positive on the read that reaches the end, and the MCU loop stops at the next
row on `overread_by > 0` alone. This counts the zeros appended once the data has ended and stops
a row only once they, or more bits than the buffer held, have been consumed. A frame with an EOI
decodes to the same bytes as before (27 frames, lenient and strict); over every truncation of
fifteen cjpeg fixtures the patched decoder never yields fewer rows equal to the whole frame's, and
the most rows that are neither those nor 128 at any truncation is the same; a whole decode's
instruction count does not rise (callgrind, 510.2 M before and 509.0 M after on a 4 MB photograph). The reproduction is the 348-byte codestream
below (hexadecimal), decoded whole: lines 104 to 106 are 128 without the patch and equal the same
codestream's decode with FF D9 appended with it. `crates/pdf-model/tests/banded_decodes.rs`'s
`a_complete_last_row_without_its_eoi_is_decoded_as_the_frame` holds the two decodes equal.
